<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Baden-Powell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Robert Stephenson Smyth Baden-Powell, Lord Baden-Powell, Stephe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Baden-Powell, 1st Baron Baden-Powell, was a British Army officer and writer who founded The Boy Scouts Association and, with his sister Agnes, The Girl Guides Association. He served in the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from 1876 until 1910, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> in 1899 he defended the town in the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahikeng">Mafeking</a>. In August 1907 he held an experimental camp on Brownsea Island to test his ideas for training boys, and in 1908 he published Scouting for Boys, which helped the rapid growth of the Scout Movement. He retired from guiding the two associations in 1937 and died in Nyeri, Kenya, in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-baden-powell-1st-baron-baden-powell.jpg" alt="Robert Baden-Powell" width="330" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Russell &amp; Sons [ 1 ]; derivative work by Robertus Pius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Baden-Powell,_Bain_news_service_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first instalment of <b>Robert Baden-Powell</b>'s handbook on outdoor skills and citizenship appeared in London.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
